{"nodes": [{"id": "mcp_server_readme_using_this_from_an_agent", "label": "Using this from an agent (MCP server README)", "file_type": "document", "source_file": "mcp-server/README.md", "source_location": "L1-L24", "source_url": null, "captured_at": null, "author": null, "contributor": null, "rationale": "Each week ends with a working pipeline, and this folder puts those pipelines behind an MCP server so an agent that already talks to GTM tools can hand Jev the decisions. Deciding is where the bill goes: every row the agent looks at costs frontier model tokens, and most of those decisions are the typed kind Jev does at a fraction of the price and a tenth of the latency."}, {"id": "mcp_server_readme_enrichment_server", "label": "Enrichment MCP server (headcount, titles, funding)", "file_type": "concept", "source_file": "mcp-server/README.md", "source_location": "L15-L17", "source_url": null, "captured_at": null, "author": null, "contributor": null, "rationale": "Runs before triage_lead in the inbound flow. Its headcount should be passed through, because segment reads headcount directly and a lead that arrives with headcount gets segmented from a number instead of a guess."}, {"id": "mcp_server_readme_crm_server", "label": "CRM MCP server", "file_type": "concept", "source_file": "mcp-server/README.md", "source_location": "L15-L17", "source_url": null, "captured_at": null, "author": null, "contributor": null, "rationale": "Where inbound form fills land, and where the agent sets owner and stage from the route triage_lead returns."}, {"id": "mcp_server_readme_slack_server", "label": "Slack MCP server", "file_type": "concept", "source_file": "mcp-server/README.md", "source_location": "L15-L17", "source_url": null, "captured_at": null, "author": null, "contributor": null, "rationale": "Where the agent drafts first emails for reps in the inbound flow and posts owner notes after the Monday risk sweep."}, {"id": "mcp_server_readme_uv_inline_dependencies", "label": "uv run with inline script dependencies", "file_type": "concept", "source_file": "mcp-server/README.md", "source_location": "L28-L30", "source_url": null, "captured_at": null, "author": null, "contributor": null, "rationale": "jev_server.py and check.py declare `mcp>=2.2,<3` in a `# /// script` header, so `uv run` fetches the MCP SDK on first run and there is nothing to install. The benchmarks themselves use only the Python standard library."}, {"id": "mcp_server_readme_ledger_path_restriction", "label": "JEV_LEDGER_DIR path restriction on deals_at_risk", "file_type": "rationale", "source_file": "mcp-server/README.md", "source_location": "L39-L42", "source_url": null, "captured_at": null, "author": null, "contributor": null, "rationale": "deals_at_risk resolves the requested name under LEDGER_DIR and refuses anything that is not a file inside it, including ../../.env.local, where the API key lives. check.py asks for that path and exits unless the call comes back as an error."}, {"id": "mcp_server_readme_warm_connection_latency", "label": "Warm connection latency (979 ms first call, 360 ms median)", "file_type": "concept", "source_file": "mcp-server/README.md", "source_location": "L58-L61", "source_url": null, "captured_at": null, "author": null, "contributor": null, "rationale": "The first call on a fresh connection pays for the TLS handshake. Eight triage_lead calls in a row through the server took 979 ms for the first and a median of 360 ms for the rest. ask() keeps one warm HTTPS connection per thread."}, {"id": "mcp_server_readme_tools_load_week_specs", "label": "Tools load their questions from each week's spec.py", "file_type": "rationale", "source_file": "mcp-server/README.md", "source_location": "L63-L66", "source_url": null, "captured_at": null, "author": null, "contributor": null, "rationale": "Change a prompt in a week's spec.py and the tool changes with it, so a benchmark number in the repo describes what the tool does, as long as the benchmark is re-run after the change. load() imports each spec.py by file path under its own name, because both weeks call the module spec.py and a plain import would return whichever loaded first."}, {"id": "mcp_server_readme_inbound_lead_workflow", "label": "Inbound lead workflow (enrichment, triage_lead, CRM, Slack)", "file_type": "concept", "source_file": "mcp-server/README.md", "source_location": "L73-L90", "source_url": null, "captured_at": null, "author": null, "contributor": null, "rationale": "A form fill lands in the CRM, the enrichment server adds headcount, title and company type, triage_lead returns disqualify, icp_fit, segment, intent and a route, the CRM server sets owner and stage from the route, and only ae_now or sdr_sequence leads get an agent-written first email drafted in Slack. Enrichment goes first because segment reads headcount directly. In week 01, 29 of 60 leads earned an email and the other 31 cost about four thousandths of a cent each to reject or nurture."}, {"id": "mcp_server_readme_monday_risk_sweep", "label": "Monday risk sweep workflow (activity database, deals_at_risk, Slack)", "file_type": "concept", "source_file": "mcp-server/README.md", "source_location": "L92-L115", "source_url": null, "captured_at": null, "author": null, "contributor": null, "rationale": "The activity database exports the week's ledger to JEV_LEDGER_DIR, deals_at_risk checks every deal and returns only the risky ones, the agent checks each flag against the ledger and writes a note to each owner, and the Slack server posts them. Given a plain request, Claude Code called deals_at_risk once and returned all eight at-risk deals from the week 02 answer key ($1.145M, correct reasons), then added that Precision is the smallest deal and the most urgent because it closes in three days. Jev made 219 small decisions and the agent never opened the ledger."}, {"id": "mcp_server_readme_fan_out_inside_tool", "label": "Do the fan-out inside the tool", "file_type": "rationale", "source_file": "mcp-server/README.md", "source_location": "L117-L137", "source_url": null, "captured_at": null, "author": null, "contributor": null, "rationale": "Letting the agent read the ledger and call deal_risk once per deal works, but every event passes through the agent's context on the way in. On the week 02 ledger the file is 20,042 bytes and what deals_at_risk hands back is 669 bytes, for 20 deals. The ledger grows with events and the answer only with deals at risk. So the tool reads the file itself, fans out to Jev on eight threads, applies the rule in code, and returns only what someone should act on."}, {"id": "mcp_server_readme_tool_description_fix", "label": "Tool description fix (agent searched the filesystem)", "file_type": "rationale", "source_file": "mcp-server/README.md", "source_location": "L141-L148", "source_url": null, "captured_at": null, "author": null, "contributor": null, "rationale": "The first deals_at_risk description said the file lived inside the directory set by JEV_LEDGER_DIR. Claude took that as an instruction to find it and made four shell calls first, one a `find /` across the whole disk. The description now says to pass just the file name and that the server opens it, which took the run from 7 turns to 4 and cut searching to a single `ls`. The description is the only instruction an agent gets about a tool."}, {"id": "mcp_server_readme_toolerror_for_expected_failures", "label": "ToolError for expected failures instead of ValueError", "file_type": "rationale", "source_file": "mcp-server/README.md", "source_location": "L150-L154", "source_url": null, "captured_at": null, "author": null, "contributor": null, "rationale": "Raising ValueError from the path check made the SDK log a traceback and send the agent a blank failure it could not learn from. ToolError from mcp.server.mcpserver.exceptions is for failures you saw coming: the agent gets the message and can fix its call."}, {"id": "mcp_server_readme_mcp_sdk_2x_migration", "label": "MCP SDK 2.x move (FastMCP became MCPServer)", "file_type": "concept", "source_file": "mcp-server/README.md", "source_location": "L156-L158", "source_url": null, "captured_at": null, "author": null, "contributor": null, "rationale": "In mcp 2.x FastMCP became MCPServer in mcp.server.mcpserver, and results use structured_content and is_error where 1.x used camelCase. The server pins mcp>=2.2,<3."}, {"id": "mcp_server_readme_typeddict_structured_output", "label": "TypedDict returns for structured tool output", "file_type": "rationale", "source_file": "mcp-server/README.md", "source_location": "L160-L166", "source_url": null, "captured_at": null, "author": null, "contributor": null, "rationale": "The SDK decides whether a tool has structured output from its return annotation, and `-> dict` does not count. Results still arrived as JSON text, which hid the problem. Each tool now returns a TypedDict (Lead, DealRisk, Sweep), so it has an output schema and clients get structured content. check.py exits if structured content is missing, because the first version quietly fell back to parsing text."}, {"id": "mcp_server_readme_no_agent_date_arithmetic", "label": "Hand the agent day counts instead of date arithmetic", "file_type": "rationale", "source_file": "mcp-server/README.md", "source_location": "L168-L171", "source_url": null, "captured_at": null, "author": null, "contributor": null, "rationale": "In week 02, Claude reviewing Jev's flags decided an August 15 meeting fell inside a 30 day window ending September 25, while getting everything else right. An agent that reviews what these tools return should be given the numbers and never asked to count days."}, {"id": "mcp_server_readme_per_call_cost", "label": "Per-call cost, Jev against Sonnet 5", "file_type": "concept", "source_file": "mcp-server/README.md", "source_location": "L173-L184", "source_url": null, "captured_at": null, "author": null, "contributor": null, "rationale": "One lead through triage_lead costs $0.00004 on Jev against $0.0030 for the same work on Sonnet 5. One deal through deal_risk costs $0.00012 against $0.0040. A million ledger events a day runs about $1,255 a month against about $70,500. The agent's own tokens come on top, and the fan-out rule keeps those small."}, {"id": "mcp_server_readme_week_02_rule_thresholds", "label": "Week 02 rule thresholds ($100K exec rule, 30 days, 7 days to close)", "file_type": "rationale", "source_file": "mcp-server/README.md", "source_location": "L188-L191", "source_url": null, "captured_at": null, "author": null, "contributor": null, "rationale": "The tools apply the week 02 rule as written. The thresholds suit the synthetic pipeline in the repo and a real pipeline will want its own. The README points to the top of week-02-deal-risk/src/spec.py, but the numbers are inline in assess() and repeated in the RISK_RULES text."}, {"id": "mcp_server_readme_ledger_jsonl_input_shape", "label": "Ledger JSONL input shape (one deal per line with its events)", "file_type": "concept", "source_file": "mcp-server/README.md", "source_location": "L193-L195", "source_url": null, "captured_at": null, "author": null, "contributor": null, "rationale": "deals_at_risk reads JSONL in the shape the week 02 ledger uses. Whatever an activity database exports needs a small step that reshapes it before the sweep."}, {"id": "mcp_server_readme_made_up_competitor_list", "label": "Competitor list in the prompt (Hexline, Corvid, Tallyworks)", "file_type": "rationale", "source_file": "mcp-server/README.md", "source_location": "L197-L199", "source_url": null, "captured_at": null, "author": null, "contributor": null, "rationale": "The competitor names in the week 02 prompt are made up and should be replaced with real ones, because week 01 showed Jev will not work out who a company's competitors are on its own."}], "edges": [{"source": "mcp_server_readme_using_this_from_an_agent", "target": "mcp_server_jev_server", "relation": "references", "confidence": "EXTRACTED", "confidence_score": 1.0, "so